# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): club practice and job shifts.
# Clubs: duelling, lumen_ledger, verdant, cartographers. Jobs: library_assistant, tavern, apothecary_clerk, broom_courier.
# Each track: three repeatable routines (two at rank 1, one at rank 2) and one rank-2 milestone.

# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------

@event duelling_ward_line
title: The Ward Line
track: duelling
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: shield
tags: club duel
scene: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.
text: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}
> left: Take the front of the line
  challenge spell apprentice+:
    win:
      perform duelling +small
      vigor +small
      say: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}
    lose:
      health -small
      perform duelling +tiny
      say: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}
> right: Hold the back row tonight
  arcana +small
  perform duelling -tiny
  say: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}
> up[ward]: Hold the line with a real ward
  perform duelling +small
  arcana +small
  say: {You raise a proper ward, the one from your first week, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}

@event duelling_dummy_mending
title: Mending the Dummies
track: duelling
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: duel
tags: club
scene: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.
text: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}
> left: Stay late and stitch them up
  mood -small
  perform duelling +small
  rank_xp duelling +tiny
  say: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}
> right: Leave them for the morning
  mood +tiny
  perform duelling -tiny
  say: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}

@event duelling_refereeing_lyra
title: Refereeing Lyra
track: duelling
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
actor: lyra
when: around(lyra)
art: duel
tags: club duel
scene: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.
text: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}
> left: Call it against her
  rel lyra -small
  alignment +tiny
  perform duelling +small
  rank_xp duelling +small
  say: {"Noted," Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}
> right: Let it stand
  rel lyra +tiny
  alignment -tiny
  perform duelling -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}

@event duelling_running_practice
title: Running Practice
track: duelling
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: duel
tags: club duel
scene: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.
text: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: "Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again." The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.
> left: Run it exactly her way
  perform duelling +small
  rank_xp duelling +medium
  rel lyra +small
  say: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says "adequate", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.
> right: Run it your own way
  rank_xp duelling +medium
  renown +small
  mood +small
  say: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.

# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger (track: lumen_ledger) ----------------

@event lumen_ledger_letters_box
title: The Letters Box
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: club
scene: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.
text: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}
> left: Print the angriest letter
  perform lumen_ledger +small
  renown +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls "engagement".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}
> right: Answer them all by hand
  charm +small
  mood -small
  perform lumen_ledger +tiny
  say: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it "a waste of a perfectly good grudge", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}

@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes
title: A Word from the Courtyard
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: club social
scene: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.
text: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == "winter": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}
> left: Stop strangers and ask
  challenge appeal apprentice:
    win:
      perform lumen_ledger +small
      charm +small
      say: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      perform lumen_ledger +tiny
      say: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with "no comment" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is "No."}
> right: Just quote your friends
  mood +tiny
  perform lumen_ledger -tiny
  say: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, "Interesting. Your friends are very consistent."|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}

@event lumen_ledger_red_ink
title: Red Ink
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
actor: nessa
art: scroll
tags: club
scene: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.
text: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} "{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}" She has cut your favourite paragraph.
> left: Fight for the paragraph
  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm:
    win:
      perform lumen_ledger +small
      rank_xp lumen_ledger +small
      say: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. "Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it."|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it "a paragraph now".}
    lose:
      mood -small
      rel nessa -tiny
      say: {"No," says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}
> right: Let her cut it
  perform lumen_ledger +small
  mood -small
  say: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles "good" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}

@event lumen_ledger_front_page
title: Front Page
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: paper
tags: club
scene: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.
text: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. "Wall," she says. "That's where front pages go."
> left: Pin it to the wall yourself
  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium
  perform lumen_ledger +small
  rel nessa +small
  say: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.
> right: Send the first copy home
  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium
  mood +medium
  gold -tiny
  say: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.

# ---------------- Verdant Circle (track: verdant) ----------------

@event verdant_watering_rota
title: The Watering Rota
track: verdant
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: leaf
tags: club nature
scene: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.
text: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == "winter": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == "summer": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}
> left: Follow the chart exactly
  perform verdant +small
  alchemy +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look "right pleased", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}
> right: Water by feel
  challenge research using alchemy apprentice:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      perform verdant +small
      say: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      perform verdant -tiny
      say: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}

@event verdant_repotting_day
title: Repotting Day
track: verdant
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: flower
tags: club nature
scene: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.
text: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == "spring": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}
> left: Take on the mandrakes
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  perform verdant +small
  rank_xp verdant +tiny
  say: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}
> right: Take the quiet seed trays
  alchemy +small
  health -tiny
  perform verdant +tiny
  say: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}

@event verdant_tobias_cuttings
title: The Cuttings Question
track: verdant
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
actor: tobias
when: around(tobias)
art: leaf
tags: club nature
scene: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.
text: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. "It's like choosing between goats," he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == "autumn": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}
> left: Choose, and compost the rest
  perform verdant +small
  rank_xp verdant +small
  rel tobias -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says "fair's fair", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}
> right: Find room for every one
  rel tobias +small
  perform verdant -tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you," Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}

@event verdant_greenhouse_keys
title: The Greenhouse Keys
track: verdant
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
actor: tobias
when: around(tobias)
art: key
tags: club nature
scene: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.
text: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. "Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked," he says. "You always shut the gate." From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.
> left: Open up at dawn
  rank_xp verdant +medium
  perform verdant +small
  health -tiny
  rel tobias +small
  say: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.
> right: Walk the night round with a lamp
  rank_xp verdant +medium
  alchemy +small
  mood +small
  say: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.

# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers (track: cartographers) ----------------

@event cartographers_survey_run
title: A Survey Run
track: cartographers
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: map
tags: club mystery
scene: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.
text: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}
> left: Pace it properly
  challenge research using guile apprentice:
    win:
      guile +small
      say: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}
> right: Copy last month's figures
  alignment -tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. "Forger," he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}

@event cartographers_lantern_patrol
title: The Prefect's Lantern
track: cartographers
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: club mystery
scene: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.
text: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}
> left: Freeze in the shadows
  challenge deceive apprentice+:
    win:
      guile +tiny
      say: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}
> right: Go back the long way
  guile +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}

@event cartographers_inking_night
title: Inking Night
track: cartographers
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
actor: corvin
art: map
tags: club
scene: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.
text: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {"Leave the one behind the kitchens off," he says. "Not for long. For me."|"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why."|He taps a corridor. "That one stays off the map. Humour me."}
> left: Ink it anyway
  rel corvin -small
  alignment +tiny
  say: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. "Saint," he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}
> right: Leave it off for him
  rel corvin +small
  say: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}

@event cartographers_silver_pen
title: A Pen of Your Own
track: cartographers
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
actor: corvin
art: map
tags: club
scene: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.
text: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. "Mappers carry their own," he says. "And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule." Tonight you found one.
> left: Name it after yourself
  guile +small
  mood +small
  say: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.
> right: Name it for a club joke
  rel corvin +small
  say: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. "Keeper material," he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.

# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------

@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves
title: The Wandering Section
track: library_assistant
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: book
tags: job study
scene: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.
text: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}
> left: Hunt it down by catalogue
  wage
  challenge research apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      lore +small
      perform library_assistant +small
      say: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      perform library_assistant -tiny
      say: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}
> right: Put up a sign and wait
  wage
  mood +tiny
  perform library_assistant -tiny
  say: {"Back shortly," says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}

@event library_assistant_hush_round
title: The Hush Round
track: library_assistant
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: job
scene: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.
text: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}
> left: Hush them firmly
  wage
  mood -tiny
  perform library_assistant +small
  rank_xp library_assistant +tiny
  say: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: "sorry", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}
> right: Let them be
  wage
  mood +tiny
  perform library_assistant -tiny
  say: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}

@event library_assistant_reference_desk
title: The Reference Desk
track: library_assistant
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: scroll
tags: job study
scene: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.
text: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants "the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth".|A first-year wants the book their professor "definitely mentioned".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}
> left: Dig until you find it
  wage
  challenge research journeyman+:
    win:
      lore +small
      perform library_assistant +small
      rank_xp library_assistant +tiny
      say: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}
> right: Send them off with a good guess
  wage
  mood +tiny
  perform library_assistant -tiny
  say: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|"Try the fourth floor." They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}

@event library_assistant_stack_keys
title: The Stack Keys
track: library_assistant
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: key
tags: job study
scene: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.
text: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. "Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them."
> left: Do the closing round alone
  wage
  rank_xp library_assistant +medium
  lore +small
  mood +small
  say: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.
> right: Let Footnote come along
  wage
  rank_xp library_assistant +medium
  mood +medium
  say: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.

# ---------------- Server at the Gilded Newt (track: tavern) ----------------

@event tavern_cider_rush
title: The Cider Rush
track: tavern
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: job money
scene: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.
text: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == "winter": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}
> left: Keep every order in your head
  wage
  challenge research apprentice:
    win:
      gold +tiny
      perform tavern +small
      say: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      perform tavern -tiny
      say: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}
> right: Write every order down
  wage
  perform tavern +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}

@event tavern_regulars_corner
title: The Regulars' Corner
track: tavern
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: group
tags: job social
scene: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.
text: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed "exactly as before".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}
> left: Sit with them a while
  wage
  charm +small
  gold +tiny
  perform tavern -tiny
  say: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}
> right: Keep the trays moving
  wage
  perform tavern +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you "the brisk one" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}

@event tavern_tab_book
title: The Tab Book
track: tavern
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
cast:
  regular: present(place) where not tagged(regular, faculty)
art: coin
tags: job money
scene: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.
text: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}
> left: Call the tab in
  wage
  gold +tiny
  perform tavern +small
  rel regular -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}
> right: Let it ride another week
  wage
  rel regular +small
  perform tavern -tiny
  say: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}

@event tavern_behind_the_bar
title: Behind the Bar
track: tavern
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: cup
tags: job
scene: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.
text: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. "You pour from tonight," she says. "Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one." The hearth crackles, as if listening.
> left: Bank the fire at closing
  wage
  rank_xp tavern +medium
  perform tavern +small
  health -tiny
  say: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.
> right: Pour the staff a round
  wage
  rank_xp tavern +medium
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means "well poured" in a language older than the Newt.

# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk (track: apothecary_clerk) ----------------

@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops
title: Counting Drops
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: bottle
tags: job
scene: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.
text: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}
> left: Count aloud, every drop
  wage
  perform apothecary_clerk +small
  alchemy +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}
> right: Count by eye, faster
  wage
  challenge brew apprentice wild:
    win:
      perform apothecary_clerk +small
      gold +tiny
      say: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}
    lose:
      perform apothecary_clerk -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}

@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check
title: The Thousand Drawers
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: job mystery
scene: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.
text: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == "winter": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}
> left: Open the odd drawer
  wage
  challenge research using alchemy apprentice wild:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      perform apothecary_clerk +small
      say: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: "Don't." You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}
> right: Sign it off unchecked
  wage
  alignment -tiny
  mood +tiny
  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny
  say: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|"Checked," you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}

@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice
title: Asking the Dispenser
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: job
scene: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.
text: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}
> left: Prescribe it yourself
  wage
  challenge brew journeyman:
    win:
      perform apothecary_clerk +small
      rank_xp apothecary_clerk +small
      say: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      perform apothecary_clerk -tiny
      say: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}
> right: Fetch one of the daughters
  wage
  alchemy +small
  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny
  say: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}

@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen
title: The Labelling Pen
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: feather
tags: job
scene: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.
text: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. "New stock," he says. "Your hand from now on. Neat, mind."
> left: Label the new drawers yourself
  wage
  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium
  alchemy +small
  perform apothecary_clerk +small
  say: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.
> right: Ask him to watch the first one
  wage
  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium
  mood +medium
  say: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.

# ---------------- Broom Courier (track: broom_courier) ----------------

@event broom_courier_morning_crossing
title: The Lake Crossing
track: broom_courier
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: broom
tags: job
scene: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.
text: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == "winter": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == "summer": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}
> left: Fly straight across
  wage
  challenge feat apprentice wild:
    win:
      perform broom_courier +small
      gold +tiny
      say: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}
    lose:
      health -small
      perform broom_courier -tiny
      say: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}
> right: Take the long way round
  wage
  perform track -tiny
  say: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}

@event broom_courier_broom_scramble
title: The Broom Scramble
track: broom_courier
rank: 1
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: broom
tags: job
scene: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.
text: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == "winter": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}
> left: Shoulder in for the good broom
  wage
  perform broom_courier +small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}
> right: Take the difficult one
  wage
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  perform broom_courier +tiny
  say: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}

@event broom_courier_dead_letters
title: The Dead-Letter Shelf
track: broom_courier
rank: 2
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: job mystery
scene: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.
text: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower"|"whoever plays the flute at dawn"|"my brother, the one who never writes"}.
> left: Track down the recipient
  wage
  challenge research apprentice+:
    win:
      perform broom_courier +small
      rank_xp broom_courier +tiny
      say: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}
> right: Leave it on the shelf
  wage
  mood +tiny
  perform broom_courier -tiny
  say: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}

@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole
title: Your Own Pigeonhole
track: broom_courier
rank: 2
class: milestone
tone: warm
weight: 20
art: bell
tags: job
scene: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.
text: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. "Your route from now on," the dispatcher says. "Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer." The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.
> left: Fly the route at once
  wage
  rank_xp broom_courier +medium
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.
> right: Stand the runners a round
  wage
  rank_xp broom_courier +medium
  mood +small
  gold -tiny
  say: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.
